\[
\begin{cases}
x'(t) = -3y(t) \\[5pt]
y'(t) = 3x(t),
\end{cases}
\qquad \begin{bmatrix} x(2) \\ y(2) \end{bmatrix} =  \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0  \end{bmatrix} 
\]
Find the solution to the initial value problem above.</p>
<p><em>Hint: $e^{At} = \begin{bmatrix} \cos(3t) & -\sin(3t) \\ \sin(3t) & \cos(3t) \end{bmatrix},$ where $A = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & -3 \\ 3 & 0 \end{bmatrix}.$</em>
